1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,600
Teach an AI something new... and it forgets something old.

2
00:00:02,850 --> 00:00:05,250
But this tiny model knows what's its own. No router.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:06,600
The secret? Compression.

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,800
What if forgetting is just a routing problem?

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,933
Today's idea comes from a short technical report by an independent researcher.

6
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:21,333
Its claim: how well a model compresses your data already tells you who should handle it.

7
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:26,650
Large language models run into three stubborn problems.

8
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:31,867
One: the context window is finite, so long documents simply don't fit.

9
00:00:31,867 --> 00:00:36,150
Two: inference is expensive, often a whole rack of GPUs.

10
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:42,617
Three: catastrophic forgetting. Fine-tune on something new, and old skills quietly erode.

11
00:00:43,817 --> 00:00:50,217
That third one is why updating a giant model means re-training on mountains of old data.

12
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:55,967
Mixture-of-Experts models help: split the work across specialists.

13
00:00:55,967 --> 00:01:02,033
But who decides which expert gets which input? A separately trained gating network.

14
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:07,250
It's one more thing to train, and its decisions are hard to interpret.

15
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:13,267
So here's the question: does routing really need to be learned at all?

16
00:01:14,667 --> 00:01:19,017
Start with a popular slogan: compression is intelligence.

17
00:01:19,017 --> 00:01:22,967
A good language model predicts the next token well.

18
00:01:22,967 --> 00:01:28,767
And if you can predict, you can compress: predictable text needs very few bits.

19
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:32,067
Unfamiliar, chaotic text needs many more.

20
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:36,483
Prediction and compression are two sides of the same coin.

21
00:01:37,483 --> 00:01:40,983
But there's a quieter corollary hiding here.

22
00:01:40,983 --> 00:01:46,583
A compressor is only good at compressing the kind of data it was trained on.

23
00:01:46,583 --> 00:01:53,050
So how well it compresses something tells you whether that something is 'one of its own'.

24
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:58,783
This report turns that intuition into an architecture: compression is routing.

25
00:01:59,583 --> 00:02:06,183
To test it, the author trained an 87-million-parameter Transformer autoencoder, end to end.

26
00:02:06,183 --> 00:02:09,083
The input is a block of 512 tokens.

27
00:02:09,083 --> 00:02:14,367
The encoder squeezes it, through attention, into just 8 latent vectors.

28
00:02:16,867 --> 00:02:21,150
512 down to 8: a 64-fold compression of sequence length.

29
00:02:21,150 --> 00:02:27,817
The decoder then rebuilds the original sequence, token by token, from those 8 vectors alone.

30
00:02:30,117 --> 00:02:37,117
The key is strict isolation. Besides z, the decoder only gets a signal m that carries no content.

31
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:45,317
No shortcut to the original: every bit of information must squeeze through that 8-vector bottleneck.

32
00:02:46,317 --> 00:02:53,250
That forces the encoder to learn the structure of the data itself, not just pass a signal along.

33
00:02:54,250 --> 00:02:59,800
Training data: source code only, from codeparrot, with the GPT-2 tokenizer.

34
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:05,683
How do we score a reconstruction? With a deliberately strict metric.

35
00:03:05,683 --> 00:03:08,650
Say the original is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5...

36
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:12,083
...and the reconstruction is 1, 2, 6, 7, 5.

37
00:03:12,083 --> 00:03:16,767
Compare position by position: 1, 2 and 5 match; 3 and 4 don't.

38
00:03:16,767 --> 00:03:19,333
Three out of five: 60 percent.

39
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:24,283
That's token-level reconstruction accuracy, or TRA.

40
00:03:24,283 --> 00:03:28,767
It only counts exact matches. 'Close enough' earns nothing.

41
00:03:29,567 --> 00:03:31,817
Now, the moment of truth.

42
00:03:32,817 --> 00:03:38,417
On held-out code it has never seen: 99.47 percent of tokens rebuilt exactly.

43
00:03:38,417 --> 00:03:43,300
Switch to English Wikipedia: accuracy collapses to 47.76 percent.

44
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,800
And on random token sequences: 0.57 percent.

45
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:53,200
Note that 47.76 is not a guess. Guessing would score about two thousandths of a percent.

46
00:03:53,200 --> 00:04:01,917
The model is exploiting what code and English share: vocabulary and basic statistics. The two distributions partly overlap.

47
00:04:02,917 --> 00:04:09,183
And random noise is flatly rejected. The model did not degenerate into a copy machine.

48
00:04:09,183 --> 00:04:15,117
One compressor, three distributions, three steps. The gap is huge and systematic.

49
00:04:15,917 --> 00:04:21,850
So reconstruction error works as a built-in fingerprint of where data comes from.

50
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:28,267
Why is the gap so large? Look at the shape of the latent vectors z.

51
00:04:28,267 --> 00:04:32,683
Project code and Wikipedia latents down to two dimensions:

52
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:38,933
Two dense, disconnected islands, with empty space between them.

53
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:43,950
A single straight line separates them. They are linearly separable.

54
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:49,617
So the routing decision is nearly free. No elaborate gating network required.

55
00:04:49,617 --> 00:04:56,617
And since each distribution lives in its own region, training one expert barely disturbs another.

56
00:04:56,617 --> 00:05:00,967
PCA on the code latents: z has 512 dimensions on paper...

57
00:05:02,467 --> 00:05:08,400
...but about 200 principal components already explain 95 percent of the variance.

58
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:14,067
The encoder has learned to drop noise and keep only the skeleton of the data.

59
00:05:14,867 --> 00:05:19,083
Put it all together and you get compression as routing.

60
00:05:19,083 --> 00:05:24,167
Give each expert its own compressor, trained only on its own domain.

61
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:27,400
A new input arrives. Say, a bit of code.

62
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:31,283
Every compressor tries to compress and rebuild it.

63
00:05:31,283 --> 00:05:34,717
Then compare their reconstruction accuracy.

64
00:05:34,717 --> 00:05:39,333
Whoever rebuilds it best gets the job. That's the whole rule.

65
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:46,533
No gating network, no extra parameters. The error itself is the routing signal, and you can read it.

66
00:05:47,533 --> 00:05:54,600
Now the part that matters for forgetting. What if a whole new domain shows up, like medical notes?

67
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:59,283
Nobody recognizes it. Every compressor reconstructs it poorly.

68
00:05:59,283 --> 00:06:03,183
That is itself a signal: time to add a new expert.

69
00:06:04,183 --> 00:06:09,917
Mount a medical compressor and train only that. Every old expert stays frozen.

70
00:06:09,917 --> 00:06:17,300
Old knowledge is physically untouched, so it can't be overwritten. Forgetting is sidestepped by design.

71
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:24,300
Instead of a monolith you re-forge every time, you get an ecosystem that grows by adding modules.

72
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:28,400
There's also an unexpected bonus: memory.

73
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,550
A standard Transformer's KV cache grows linearly with context length.

74
00:06:33,550 --> 00:06:38,500
In latent space, 512 positions become 8 vectors: 64 times shorter.

75
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,983
The paper's vision: contexts that once needed several A100s

76
00:06:42,983 --> 00:06:46,550
might fit on a single consumer graphics card.

77
00:06:47,350 --> 00:06:53,017
To be clear, this is a foundational proof of concept, and it has real limits.

78
00:06:53,017 --> 00:06:57,833
First, hysteresis. The compression window is a fixed 512 tokens.

79
00:06:57,833 --> 00:07:03,250
When the content switches mid-window, say from a novel into a code block,

80
00:07:03,250 --> 00:07:08,850
z is still dominated by what came before, so the error spikes at the switch.

81
00:07:09,850 --> 00:07:16,050
Second, real text is often interleaved, so one window can straddle two distributions.

82
00:07:16,050 --> 00:07:22,517
Third, compute was limited. At this setting, 8 vectors is the smallest length that works.

83
00:07:22,517 --> 00:07:27,600
Early experiments suggest even 128-fold compression may be possible.

84
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:35,450
And the forgetting story is an architectural argument; a full continual-learning benchmark is still open work.

85
00:07:35,450 --> 00:07:42,517
The author conjectures a scaling law, rooted in entropy, linking latent length to sequence length.

86
00:07:42,517 --> 00:07:48,717
The same three-step pattern also showed up on Chinese novels with a custom tokenizer.

87
00:07:49,517 --> 00:07:51,517
99.47. 47.76. 0.57.

88
00:07:51,517 --> 00:07:57,583
Three numbers that say: how well you compress something tells you whose data it is.

89
00:07:58,583 --> 00:08:03,200
From 'compression is prediction' to 'compression is routing'.

90
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:10,267
The author frames this as a starting point, and invites labs with more compute to push it further.

91
00:08:10,267 --> 00:08:16,133
The paper and everything else are in the repo linked below. Thanks for watching.
